Step 1: Windows VM Setup

A Windows 10 virtual machine was created in VirtualBox to act as the monitored endpoint for this SOC lab.

The VM was renamed to:

WIN-SOC-01

This hostname makes the logs easier to identify in Splunk.

Step 2: Sysmon Installation

Sysmon was downloaded from Microsoft Sysinternals and placed in the following directory:

C:\Tools

The folder included the Sysmon executable and configuration file:

Sysmon64.exe
sysmonconfig-export.xml

Sysmon was installed using PowerShell as Administrator:

cd C:\Tools
.\Sysmon64.exe -accepteula -i .\sysmonconfig-export.xml
Command Explanation
Command Part	Purpose
Sysmon64.exe	Runs the 64-bit Sysmon executable
-accepteula	Accepts the Sysmon license agreement
-i	Installs Sysmon as a Windows service
sysmonconfig-export.xml	Applies the Sysmon configuration file
Step 3: Verified Sysmon Service

After installation, I verified that Sysmon was running with the following command:

Get-Service Sysmon64

Expected result:

Status   Name      DisplayName
------   ----      -----------
Running  Sysmon64  Sysmon64

This confirmed that Sysmon was installed and actively running on the Windows endpoint.

Step 4: Verified Sysmon Logs in Event Viewer

I opened Windows Event Viewer and navigated to:

Applications and Services Logs
→ Microsoft
→ Windows
→ Sysmon
→ Operational

This confirmed that Sysmon events were being generated locally on the Windows VM.

Step 5: Splunk Enterprise Installation

Splunk Enterprise Free was installed on the Windows VM and accessed through the local web interface:

http://localhost:8000

Splunk was used as the SIEM/log analysis platform for this lab.

Step 6: Configured Splunk to Ingest Sysmon Logs

Splunk was configured to ingest the Sysmon Operational log.

The log source used was:

WinEventLog:Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational

If the Sysmon log source does not appear in Splunk automatically, it can be manually added using the inputs.conf file.

Location:

C:\Program Files\Splunk\etc\system\local\inputs.conf

Example configuration:

[WinEventLog://Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational]
disabled = 0
index = main
sourcetype = XmlWinEventLog
renderXml = true

After editing inputs.conf, Splunk can be restarted with:

cd "C:\Program Files\Splunk\bin"
.\splunk.exe restart
Step 7: Verified Sysmon Logs in Splunk

To confirm that Splunk was receiving Sysmon logs, I searched for the Sysmon log source:

index=main source="WinEventLog:Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational"

The search returned events from:

host = WIN-SOC-01
source = WinEventLog:Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational
sourcetype = XmlWinEventLog

This confirmed that Sysmon logs were successfully being ingested into Splunk.

Step 8: Generated a Test Process Event

To verify process creation logging, I opened PowerShell and launched Notepad:

notepad.exe

Then I searched for the event in Splunk:

index=main source="WinEventLog:Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational" notepad

The event showed that notepad.exe was launched and recorded by Sysmon.

Observed Event Details

The Notepad event included useful process creation details such as:

Field	Description
Host	The Windows endpoint that generated the event
User	The account that launched the process
Image	The executable that ran
CommandLine	The command used to launch the process
ParentImage	The process that launched the child process
EventID	The Sysmon event type

Example observed activity:

Image: C:\Windows\System32\notepad.exe
CommandLine: notepad.exe
ParentImage: C:\Windows\System32\WindowsPowerShell\v1.0\powershell.exe
User: WIN-SOC-01\soclab
EventID: 1
Result

Sysmon was successfully installed and configured on the Windows VM. Splunk successfully ingested Sysmon Operational logs, allowing endpoint activity to be searched and analyzed.

This completed the foundational logging pipeline needed for future SOC analyst detection labs.
